---
// Shared privacy-policy sections for the apps that can show personalized ads
// (Table Talk and EconByte, from 2026-09-15). One source so the two policies
// can't drift. Facts are from the Owner decision of 2026-09-15 and the Phase 25
// app branches: Apple's tracking prompt is the ONLY opt-in (there is no in-app
// personalization switch); not authorized => npa + rdp; unknown region =>
// non-personalized; no ad requests in the EEA, UK or Switzerland.
import { SUPPORT_EMAIL, LEGAL_NAME } from '../consts';

interface Props {
  appName: string;
  /** One sentence on where this app shows ads. */
  placements: string;
  /** What stops ads in this app, e.g. "Buying Remove Ads". */
  adFreeOptions: string;
}
const { appName, placements, adFreeOptions } = Astro.props;

const partners = [
  { name: 'Google AdMob (Google)', role: 'Runs the ad auction and serves ads', links: [
    { label: 'Google Privacy Policy', href: 'https://policies.google.com/privacy' },
    { label: 'How Google uses data from apps', href: 'https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites' },
  ] },
  { name: 'AppLovin', role: 'Partner ad network', links: [{ label: 'Privacy policy', href: 'https://www.applovin.com/privacy/' }] },
  { name: 'Meta Audience Network (Meta)', role: 'Partner ad network', links: [{ label: 'Privacy policy', href: 'https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy/' }] },
  { name: 'Unity Ads (Unity)', role: 'Partner ad network', links: [{ label: 'Privacy policy', href: 'https://unity.com/legal/game-player-and-app-user-privacy-policy' }] },
  { name: 'Liftoff Monetize (formerly Vungle)', role: 'Partner ad network', links: [{ label: 'Privacy policy', href: 'https://liftoff.io/privacy-policy/' }] },
  { name: 'Mintegral', role: 'Partner ad network', links: [{ label: 'Privacy policy', href: 'https://www.mintegral.com/en/privacy' }] },
  { name: 'InMobi', role: 'Partner ad network', links: [{ label: 'Privacy policy', href: 'https://www.inmobi.com/privacy-policy' }] },
  { name: 'Pangle', role: 'Partner ad network', links: [{ label: 'Privacy policy', href: 'https://www.pangleglobal.com/privacy/enduser-en' }] },
];
const cell = 'padding:8px 10px 8px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border, #222); vertical-align:top;';
const head = 'text-align:left; padding:6px 10px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border, #333);';
---
<h2 id="advertising">Advertising</h2>
<p>{placements}</p>
<p>
  Ads are served through <strong>Google AdMob</strong>. For each ad, AdMob runs an auction in which Google
  and the partner ad networks listed below can bid. Not every partner is active at every moment. The
  networks, not {LEGAL_NAME}, choose which ad you see. We don't receive your advertising identifier or any
  ad profile about you. We only see totals, such as how many ads were shown and what they earned.
</p>

<h3>Personalized ads happen only if you tap “Allow”</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>If you tap “Allow”</strong> on Apple's tracking prompt (App Tracking Transparency), ads may be
    personalized. Google and the partner networks may use your device's advertising identifier (IDFA) and
    information about your activity in other companies' apps and websites. They use it to pick ads that
    are more relevant to you and to measure how those ads perform. The app only reads the IDFA if you allow tracking.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>If you tap “Ask App Not to Track”</strong>, turn tracking off later, or tracking is unavailable
    on your device, the app can't read the IDFA. Every ad request is marked
    <strong>non-personalized</strong>, and the app turns on Google's <strong>restricted data
    processing</strong> signal, which limits how Google and its partners may use the data. Non-personalized
    ads are chosen from context, such as the app you're using and your general region, not from a profile of you.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>If the app can't tell which region your device is set to</strong>, it shows only non-personalized
    ads, even if you allowed tracking.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>No ads in the EEA, the UK or Switzerland.</strong> If your device's region (iOS Settings →
    General → Language &amp; Region) is a country in the European Economic Area (the 27 EU countries plus
    Iceland, Liechtenstein and Norway), the United Kingdom or Switzerland, {appName} doesn't request ads
    at all. This uses the region setting, not your location.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  Even non-personalized ads need some information to be delivered and counted. The networks may receive
  your IP address (which suggests your approximate location), your device model, iOS version, language,
  the app's name and version, and whether an ad was shown or tapped. They may also use an identifier that
  iOS limits to one developer's apps. They use this to show the ad, cap how often you see it, prevent fraud
  and report results. {appName} never shares your precise location, contacts, photos or anything you type
  with ad networks.
</p>
<p>{adFreeOptions} stops ads from showing.</p>

<h3>How to change your choice</h3>
<ul>
  <li>In the app, open <strong>Settings → Manage tracking</strong>. It takes you to {appName}'s page in iOS Settings, where you can turn <strong>Allow Tracking</strong> on or off.</li>
  <li>Or go to iOS <strong>Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Tracking</strong> and turn {appName} off. To stop every app from asking, turn off “Allow Apps to Request to Track”.</li>
</ul>
<p>The change applies to every ad requested afterwards.</p>

<h3>Ad partners</h3>
<div class="table-scroll" style="overflow-x:auto; margin:1rem 0;">
  <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-size:0.92rem;">
    <thead>
      <tr><th style={head}>Company</th><th style={head}>Role</th><th style={head}>Its privacy policy</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {partners.map((p) => (
        <tr>
          <td style={cell}>{p.name}</td>
          <td style={cell}>{p.role}</td>
          <td style={cell}>{p.links.map((l, i) => (<>{i > 0 && <br />}<a href={l.href} rel="noopener">{l.label}</a></>))}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>
  To ask a network about data it holds about your device, use the contact options in its privacy policy.
  Because we never receive that data, we can't access or delete it for you.
</p>

<h2 id="us-state-privacy-rights">Your US state privacy rights</h2>
<p>
  California residents have rights under the CCPA, as amended by the CPRA. Residents of Virginia, Colorado,
  Connecticut, Utah, Texas, Oregon and a growing number of other states with similar laws have comparable
  rights. This section explains how they apply to {appName}.
</p>

<h3>“Selling”, “sharing” and targeted advertising</h3>
<p>
  We don't sell personal information for money. However, if you allow tracking, the ad networks above
  receive your advertising identifier and ad-activity information so they can show personalized ads. California law
  calls this <strong>“sharing” for cross-context behavioral advertising</strong>, and it may also count as a
  <strong>“sale”</strong>. Other states call it <strong>“targeted advertising”</strong> or a “sale”.
  The categories involved are:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Identifiers:</strong> the advertising identifier (only if you allow tracking) and your IP address.</li>
  <li><strong>Internet or other electronic network activity:</strong> which ads were shown or tapped, and related technical data.</li>
  <li><strong>Approximate location:</strong> estimated from your IP address, never GPS.</li>
  <li><strong>Inferences:</strong> interests the ad networks may infer on their own.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  We don't sell or share sensitive personal information. We don't knowingly sell or share personal
  information of anyone under 16.
</p>

<h3>How to opt out</h3>
<p>
  For {appName}, turning tracking off is how you opt out of the sale or sharing of your personal
  information and of targeted advertising. Use <strong>Settings → Manage tracking</strong> in the app, or iOS
  <strong>Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Tracking</strong>. After you opt out, the app no longer reads your
  advertising identifier, requests only non-personalized ads, and turns on the restricted data processing signal. You
  don't have to contact us or prove who you are. Apps don't see a browser's Global Privacy Control signal, so use the
  iOS setting.
</p>

<h3>Your other rights</h3>
<p>
  Depending on where you live, you may have the right to know what personal information we have about you,
  and to access, correct, delete or get a copy of it. You also have the right not to be treated differently
  for using these rights. {appName} has no accounts, and the little data we receive is tied to a random ID
  rather than to you, so usually we have nothing that identifies you. You can still ask: email
  <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a> with the subject “Privacy request”. We'll reply
  within the time your state law requires, usually 45 days. We may ask for details to find the data. An
  authorized agent can make a request for you, with written permission.
</p>
<p>
  If we turn down your request, you can appeal by replying with the subject “Privacy appeal”. We'll explain
  our decision. If you're still unhappy, you can contact your state attorney general.
</p>
